An IPSec tunnel securely connects two networks over the internet by encrypting traffic between endpoints. Below is a step-by-step guide for configuring a site-to-site IPSec tunnel using IKEv1 on Cisco routers, which can be adapted for other platforms.

1. Define IKE Phase 1 Policy

This phase establishes a secure channel for negotiating IPSec parameters.

crypto isakmp policy 10
encryption aes
hash sha256
authentication pre-share
group 14
Copy
Set the pre-shared key and peer IP:

crypto isakmp key vpnuser address <peer_public_ip>
Copy
2. Configure IPSec Phase 2 Policy

This phase negotiates encryption for actual data traffic.

crypto ipsec transform-set myset esp-aes esp-sha256-hmac
Copy
3. Define Interesting Traffic with ACL

Only matching traffic will be encrypted.

access-list 100 permit ip 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 172.16.2.0 0.0.0.255
Copy
4. Create and Apply Crypto Map

Bind the IPSec settings to an interface.

crypto map mymap 10 ipsec-isakmp
set peer <peer_public_ip>
set transform-set myset
match address 100

interface GigabitEthernet0/0
ip address <local_public_ip> <subnet_mask>
crypto map mymap
Copy
1

5. Add Routing

Ensure traffic to the remote network routes through the tunnel.

ip route 172.16.2.0 255.255.255.0 <next_hop>
Copy
1

6. Verification

Check tunnel status:

show crypto isakmp sa # IKE Phase 1 status
show crypto ipsec sa # IPSec Phase 2 status
show crypto map # Crypto map details
